// @test: the explicit `|x<T>|` closure spelling is diagnosed, not left to an operator error
// @description: #463 -- a closure has no type-parameter list (`param<T>` is already the
//   annotation form, per the decision recorded on #385). `|x<T>|` parses as an annotation naming
//   a type `T` that does not exist, which surfaced as `Operator '*' is not defined for operands
//   'T' and 'Int'` plus a signature mismatch. It now names the two forms that work: a concrete
//   annotation, or no annotation with the context supplying the signature.
// @category: lambda, closure, generics, diagnostics
// @expect: fail
// @expect-error: is not a type

main()<Int> -> {
    f<fn(Int) -> Int> = |x<T>| -> x * 2
    println("f(3)=" + f(3).to_string())
    return 0
}
